{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at the end of two full years.</b> Since Scripture did not specify when these two years began, our Sages, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 89:2), said that these two years — he was required to remain imprisoned beyond what was decreed upon him — because he hung his trust upon the chief butler, as it says (Psalms 40:5), ״אַשְׁרֵי הַגֶּבֶר אֲשֶׁר שָׂם ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ וְלֹא פָנָה אֶל רְהָבִים״ — these are the Egyptians, who are called ״רַהַב״. And one may ask: what is this that it says, ״אֲשֶׁר שָׂם ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ — 'who has made HaShem his trust'? It should have said ״אֲשֶׁר שָׂם בַּה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ — 'who has placed his trust in HaShem,' as it is written (Jeremiah 17:7), ״בָּרוּךְ הַגֶּבֶר אֲשֶׁר יִבְטַח בַּה׳ וְהָיָה ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״. And besides the redundancy in that verse, the language also requires an adjustment in what is written, ״וְהָיָה ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ — 'and HaShem will be his trust.' And further it is difficult: why did he call the Egyptians ״רְהָבִים״ and not call them by their familiar name?" "And it seems to me to say this: that it is the way of the world that a man who has some advantage, some superiority over his fellow, does not remember his lesser fellow from his vantage, and will not bear his name upon his lips, out of the haughtiness of his heart. And this is the argument of the philosophers concerning the Name, blessed be He — saying that because of the greatness of His exaltedness, blessed be He, He does not attend to the lowly in all that is beneath the sphere of the moon. And to abolish this mistaken opinion, it was said (I Samuel 2:3), ״אַל תַּרְבּוּ תְדַבְּרוּ גְּבֹהָה גְבֹהָה, יֵצֵא עָתָק מִפִּיכֶם, כִּי אֵל דֵּעוֹת ה׳ וְלוֹ נִתְכְּנוּ עֲלִלוֹת״ — that is to say: do not speak, from the side that the Holy One, blessed be He, is exalted above all that is exalted, that He has no knowledge of the details of our deeds — for ״אֵל דֵּעוֹת ה׳״, that He has knowledge of them all, ״וְלוֹ נִתְכְּנוּ״, all the circumstances of a man's deeds. And in order to affirm this matter, that the Holy One, blessed be He, dwells with the crushed and lowly of spirit, the Holy One, blessed be He, joined to His great Name, blessed be He, four letters of a very small numerical value — smaller than all the letters in alef-bet. When you write יוֹ״ד הֵ״א וָא״ו הֵ״א, look at all the letters when you write them in their full spelling — you will not find any letters that amount to as small a number as these. And so our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Megillah 11a): Rabbi Yohanan said — everywhere you find the greatness of the Holy One, blessed be He, there you find His humility, etc. From Scripture, as it says (Psalms 68:5), ׳סֹלּוּ לָרֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת בְּיָהּ שְׁמוֹ׳. That is to say: praise Him ״בְּיָהּ שְׁמוֹ״ — for despite His being ״רֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת״, exalted above all, nevertheless ״יָהּ שְׁמוֹ״ — these are the letters smallest in numerical value, and the word has only two letters, namely the smallest in the combination of letters. And all this is a proof of His humility, blessed be He." "And according to this, the essence of the great Name is a proof and a basis of trust for those below, that the Holy One, blessed be He, remembers them and attends to them — so that there be no opening for the skeptic to say that because of the greatness of His exaltedness, blessed be He, ״עָזַב ה׳ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — HaShem has forsaken the earth; and that the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He, is not like the measure of flesh and blood, who does not attend to and remember one who is beneath him in rank. Concerning these two points this verse was said: concerning the first point — that one should trust in HaShem — it says ״אַשְׁרֵי הַגֶּבֶר אֲשֶׁר שָׂם ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ (Psalms 40:5), for the essence of the great Name and its smallness is his trust — that the Name, blessed be He, joins Himself to the lowly. Therefore it was not said ״בַּה׳״, because ״ה׳״ — that is, the essence of the Name — is his trust. And concerning this it says ״בָּרוּךְ הַגֶּבֶר אֲשֶׁר יִבְטַח בַּה׳״ (Jeremiah 17:7) — and in what shall he trust? Concerning this it says ״וְהָיָה ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ — the essence of the Name is his trust. And concerning the second point — that one should not trust in man, for the haughtiness of his heart brings him to forgetfulness, as it says ״וְרָם לְבָבֶךָ וְשָׁכַחְתָּ אֶת ה׳״ (Deuteronomy 8:14) — it says ״וְלֹא פָנָה אֶל רְהָבִים״ (Psalms 40:5), for ״רַהַב״ is a term of haughtiness of heart and arrogance of spirit; for one must not turn the face of his trust toward flesh and blood, for because of his arrogance and his haughtiness of spirit he does not turn toward the one who trusts in him, as it says ״וְלֹא זָכַר שַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים אֶת יוֹסֵף״ (Genesis 40:23) — because once the chief butler was restored and seated upon his pedestal and became an officer and a person of consequence, therefore he did not remember Joseph, for Joseph was in his eyes despised and a lowly sort of person. Therefore he called the Egyptians ״רְהָבִים״. And the reason it was decreed upon Joseph to be imprisoned two years is because this sin is twofold, as it is written (Jeremiah 17:5), ״אָרוּר הַגֶּבֶר אֲשֶׁר יִבְטַח בָּאָדָם וְשָׂם בָּשָׂר זְרֹעוֹ וּמִן ה׳ יָסוּר לִבּוֹ״. Therefore one year was decreed corresponding to the trust in man — for the time of forgetting is one year, as one who is forgotten from the heart after a year — and another year corresponding to ״וּמִן ה׳ יָסוּר לִבּוֹ״." "And it is possible to explain further in the verse ״וְהָיָה ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״, that there are many rungs in the trait of bitachon — our Master mentioned them in this parashah. And the last rung is the one who trusts in HaShem without any intermediary cause, who does not think to say, 'The Holy One, blessed be He, will do this thing for me by means of this cause' — for a man does not know which cause is for his good, for perhaps a man thinks that this thing is a cause for obtaining what he seeks, and it can be that it is the opposite of what he intended. Therefore it says (Psalms 37:5), ״וּבְטַח עָלָיו וְהוּא יַעֲשֶׂה״ — that is to say, the Holy One, blessed be He, will make the causes that seem good in the eyes of HaShem, blessed be He, and not you will choose among them. And had it said everywhere ״בְּטַח בַּה׳״, I would have said that he hangs his trust on some cause. Therefore it says ״וְהָיָה ה׳ מִבְטַחוֹ״ — that is to say, HaShem Himself is his trust, without any cause. And of this kind was the trust of Joseph, that without doubt he trusted in HaShem, except that he hung the cause upon the chief butler. Therefore they showed him from Heaven that this was not the cause of his redemption as he thought — but rather the counsel of HaShem, it shall stand." "<b>And behold, he stood by the river.</b> But Pharaoh said ״עַל שְׂפַת הַיְאֹר״ (Genesis 41:3). In his dream he saw seven other cows coming up after them from the river, but Pharaoh did not mention ״מִן הַיְאֹר״. In his dream it says ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה אֵצֶל הַפָּרוֹת״ (Genesis 41:3), but Pharaoh did not mention this. And according to the plain sense, he did not mention this so that Joseph should not understand that they only appeared ugly when they stood beside the beautiful ones — but when they did not stand beside them they were not so ugly, because they were only ugly in relation to the beautiful ones. Therefore Pharaoh did not mention this, so that he should understand that they were ugly in and of themselves. And in order to strengthen the matter he said here, ״לֹא רָאִיתִי כָהֵנָּה בְכָל אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם לָרֹעַ״ (Genesis 41:19), for this indicates that they appeared ugly also in and of themselves. And so Joseph interpreted this when he said ״וְנִשְׁכַּח כָּל הַשָּׂבָע״ (Genesis 41:30) — lest you say that there will not be an actual famine, but only for one who remembers the years of plenty and sees against them the lack in the years of famine; but for one who does not remember the years of plenty, the famine will not seem so great. Therefore he said ״וְנִשְׁכַּח כָּל הַשָּׂבָע״, for the years of famine were evil in themselves, even in the eyes of one who does not remember the years of plenty. Pharaoh saw ״יְפוֹת מַרְאֶה וּבְרִיאוֹת בָּשָׂר״ (Genesis 41:2), but to Joseph he said that he saw ״בְּרִיאוֹת בָּשָׂר וִיפֹת תֹּאַר״ (Genesis 41:18). And there are two changes here: namely, the change of expression from מַרְאֶה to תֹּאַר, and the reversal of the order to mention בְּרִיאוֹת בָּשָׂר first. And likewise many changes are found in the account of his dream from what he saw." "And I say: all of [the variations] can be resolved by a single proposal drawn from the words of the commentators, that Pharaoh held the Nile to be a god, and therefore he did not wish to say ״הִנְנִי עוֹמֵד עַל הַיְאוֹר״ ('here I am standing on the Nile'), because he wished to pay honor to his god. And by this all the changes mentioned will be explained, for on this account he did not wish to say that the ill-favored cows came up from the Nile, because he did not wish to attribute anything evil to his god, believing that it rules only over good things and that nothing evil issues from it. And perhaps he thought that there is another god that rules over evils, as in the view of the Manicheans that the Akeidah brought in the portion of Va'etchanan. And perhaps it is on this account that Pharaoh said (Exodus 10:10) ״רְאוּ כִּי רָעָה נֶגֶד פְּנֵיכֶם״ ('see that evil is before your faces') — that the one who rules over evils is before your faces. And this is what it says (I Samuel 2:3) ״אַל תַּרְבּוּ תְדַבְּרוּ גְּבֹהָה גְבֹהָה״ ('do not multiply speaking高高 haughty things haughty') — to say that there are two haughty ones, one ruling over good things and one over evils. Therefore it immediately says ״ה׳ מֵמִית וּמְחַיֶּה, מוֹרִישׁ וּמַעֲשִׁיר״ ('HaShem kills and brings to life, brings low and raises up') — meaning, the one who works all opposites, and all comes from Him, blessed be He. And so it says (Deuteronomy 32:39) ״רְאוּ כִּי אֲנִי אֲנִי הוּא… אֲנִי אָמִית וַאֲחַיֶּה, מָחַצְתִּי וַאֲנִי אֶרְפָּא״ ('See now that I, I am He… I kill and I bring to life, I wound and I heal'), and the doubling of ״אֲנִי אֲנִי״ parallels the doubling of ״גְּבֹהָה גְבֹהָה.״" "Now on this account, Pharaoh did not say ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה אֵצֶל הַפָּרוֹת וַתֹּאכַלְנָה״ ('and they stood beside the cows and ate'), because this would have implied as though they made war between them, until the one ruling over evils grew so great that the ill-favored cows ate the favored ones. For the expression ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה״ ('and they stood') indicates that they did not initially come to enter among the evil ones in order to satisfy them and annul their hunger, since they stood beside them. If so, their intention had been to stand, had not the evil ones overpowered them and swallowed them against their will. And this would be a disgrace to his god, that it lacked the power to save the good things flowing from it. Therefore Pharaoh changed [the wording] and said ״וַתָּבֹאנָה אֶל קִרְבֶּנָה״ ('and they came into their inward parts'), intending by this that of their own will, by the goodness of their own desire, they entered inside the evil ones to satisfy their hunger. And all this was from the measure of the goodness of his divinity, that it wished to benefit and to mend the evil ones. And if so, they did not come initially except in order to enter into their inward parts and not in order to stand there; therefore he did not say ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה אֵצֶל הַפָּרוֹת.״ And it testified that it availed nothing at all, ״כִּי לֹא נוֹדַע כִּי בָּאוּ אֶל קִרְבֶּנָה וּמַרְאֵיהֶן רַע כְּבַתְּחִלָּה״ ('for it could not be known that they had come into their inward parts, and their appearance was as bad as at first') — because they were in the extreme of evil, and therefore it was necessary to testify ״לֹא רָאִיתִי כָהֵנָּה בְּכָל אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם לָרֹעַ״ ('I have never seen the like in all the land of Egypt for badness'). And there are those who say that he mentioned this so it should not be said that before that night he had seen cows as evil as these and was struck by seeing such a novelty, and therefore this dream came to him — for such is the way, that whatever a person sees that is novel by day and ponders it, is seen by him in a dream. Therefore he said ״לֹא רָאִיתִי כָהֵנָּה בְּכָל אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם לָרֹעַ,״ and this is a proof that this dream is among the true dreams." "Now as for the matter of 'fair of appearance and fair of form' (יְפֹת מַרְאֶה וְתֹאַר), there are those who say that this does not apply to beasts but only to the human species; therefore Scripture changed from מַרְאֶה ('appearance') to תֹאַר ('form'), and therefore Rashi explained that it is a sign for creatures. But it appears to me that this is not so, for the verse itself says that they appeared to him as יְפֹת מַרְאֶה — if so, it certainly applies also to beasts. And further: would they show a person something that does not exist, such as an elephant entering the eye of a needle? Rather, it certainly applies also to beasts, and it is when all her limbs are filled with flesh and she is not emaciated — this is her beauty of appearance. And 'healthy of flesh' (בְּרִיאוֹת בָּשָׂר) is the explanation of 'fair of appearance' (יְפֹת מַרְאֶה), for without this, how would one know whether the flesh within is healthy or sick, if not by her beauty of appearance that testifies to the health of the flesh within? And the תֹאַר ('form') is another matter that does not depend on the health of the flesh, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְרָחֵל הָיְתָה יְפַת תֹּאַר וִיפַת מַרְאֶה״ (Genesis 29:17) — its meaning is the shape of the face. By way of example: if the nose is not crooked or longer than it ought to be, and likewise the eyes and lips and similar features. Now this verse says that he saw coming up from the Nile seven cows, fair of appearance and healthy of flesh — and this is a natural thing, even though the Nile has no divine power; nevertheless, its natural effect is that the water irrigates the land and causes grass to sprout for the cattle until they become fat and lush. But the Nile has no power to make them fair of form in nose and eyes and mouth, such that they would be straight and not crooked and not too large or too small — for this is the work of the El who made all His creatures straight in their form. And Pharaoh, for the honor of his god — according to his mistaken notion, for he thought there was divine power in the Nile — therefore attributed this power to his god, saying that they came up from the Nile healthy of flesh and fair of form, to tell you: not only did it bring up cows healthy of flesh, which is not such a great novelty, for all waters can do this by their nature — but it even produced fair of form, for it created them according to this design, according to his view. Therefore in this account it placed 'healthy of flesh' before 'fair of form,' in the manner of 'not only this, but even that.' And regarding the evil ones it reversed the order and said ״דַּלּוֹת וְרָעוֹת תֹּאַר וְדַקּוֹת בָּשָׂר״ ('gaunt and evil of form and thin of flesh'), to say: not only were they evil of form and their appearance altered — which is not so significant, and the absence of [divine] effect is not such a great novelty — but they were even thin of flesh, contrary to nature, and in this there is significance." "And a clear proof for our words is what Pharaoh said: ״וַתֹּאכַלְנָה הַפָּרוֹת הַדַּקּוֹת וְהָרָעוֹת אֵת שֶׁבַע פָּרוֹת הָרִאשֹׁנוֹת הַבְּרִיאוֹת״ ('and the thin and evil cows ate the seven first cows, the healthy ones') — why did he not mention the matter of form (תֹּאַר) regarding any of them, neither the good nor the evil? Rather, certainly because it makes no difference to the matter of health and eating, for they were no hungrier on account of being evil of form, but rather on account of being evil of appearance, that is, emaciated. And likewise they did not eat the good ones on account of their being fair of form, but rather on account of their being healthy of flesh, that is, fair of appearance. And what is written ״וַתֹּאכַלְנָה הַפָּרוֹת״ ('and the cows ate') and it does not say 'the seven cows' — because the seven years of famine were not consecutive, for when Jacob came after two years the famine ceased and returned at his death, as will be explained (Genesis 50:10) in the portion Vayechi at the threshing floor of Atad with the help of HaShem (Genesis 49:10)." "And as for his adding the expression ״צְנֻמוֹת״ ('withered') regarding the ears of grain — because hearing is not the same as seeing. Pharaoh saw thin ears of grain, and there are times when thin ears have thin grains in them, and Pharaoh saw with his own eyes that they were empty and contained no grains at all. And he was afraid lest Joseph mislead [him] with the expression 'thin,' for he [Joseph] had not seen them other than what he heard from Pharaoh; therefore he explained to him 'withered' — I saw that there was nothing in them but chaff. And by this all the doubts with which most of the commentators struggled will be resolved, and it is a precious thing. And the rest of the matters of the dream will be explained shortly, with the help of HaShem."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {3 "The abbreviation רבינו refers to Rashi, whom the Kli Yakar frequently cites as רבינו in his commentary.", 6 "The phrase המוֹשֵׁל עַל הָרָעוֹת ('the one who rules over evils') appears to refer back to Pharaoh's putative second deity; rendered literally throughout. The citation 'שמואל א ב ג' normalized to (I Samuel 2:3).", 7 "The Aramaic phrase פִּילָא דְעַיֵּיל בְּקוּפָא דְמַחֲטָא ('an elephant entering the eye of a needle') rendered idiomatically as it is a known rabbinic expression for the absurdly impossible.", 8 "The phrase וְרָעוֹת תֹּאַר appears in the lemma at Genesis 41:4; the Kli Yakar's text of Pharaoh's speech at 41:20 reads דַּלּוֹת וְרָעוֹת תֹּאַר. The second citation '(בראשית מט י)' likely refers to the blessing of Judah (the scepter shall not depart), associated with the cessation of famine at Jacob's death.", 9 "The clause לֹא רָאָה אוֹתָם זוּלַת מַה שֶּׁשָּׁמַע מִן פַּרְעֹה refers to Joseph; rendered 'for he had not seen them other than what he heard from Pharaoh.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:03.743591432Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}